{
  "policy_id": "grn-41-reform-the-home-office-and-simplify",
  "party": "Green",
  "policy_title": "Reform the Home Office and simplify visa applications",
  "measure": "D1",
  "verdict": {
    "measure": "D1",
    "shift": "toward more open",
    "net_migration": "raises",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "This policy moves toward a more open immigration system by making visa applications simpler, faster, and cheaper, and by removing extra NHS charges for visa holders. These changes would likely make the UK more attractive to migrants, raising net migration.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether lower fees and faster processing would increase applications enough to materially raise net migration numbers, or mainly shift the composition of arrivals.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy would simplify visa applications, process them quickly, reduce fees to cost-price, and give visa-holding migrants free NHS access.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Simplify visa applications, process them swiftly and empathetically, charge only cost-price application fees, and ensure free and comprehensive NHS access for visa-holding migrants."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Current visa fees significantly exceed the administrative cost of processing, subsidising wider Home Office activities.",
        "evidence_id": "E8",
        "quote": "The Home Office currently charges significantly more than the administrative cost of processing visas to subsidise wider immigration and border control activities"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "A five-year work visa can currently cost around \u00a310,000 in total charges.",
        "evidence_id": "E10",
        "quote": "a five-year work visa for a small business, including various charges, can cost around \u00a310,000"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Simplifying and speeding up processing would make the UK significantly more attractive to international students, skilled workers, and entrepreneurs.",
        "evidence_id": "E1",
        "quote": "Simplifying applications and speeding up processing would significantly enhance the UK's appeal to international students, skilled workers, and entrepreneurs"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy moves in the direction of more open immigration through three mechanisms: lower application costs, faster processing, and removal of the Immigration Health Surcharge for visa holders. Current fees are set well above cost-price, so reducing them to cost-price would lower a material financial barrier. Faster processing reduces uncertainty, which evidence suggests increases the UK's attractiveness to prospective migrants. Free NHS access removes a further financial disincentive. Together these changes are likely to raise net migration by increasing both the volume and the ease of legal migration routes, though the size of the effect depends on unresolved questions about demand elasticity.",
    "sources": [
